File permissions in Linux
Project description
In this project I practiced using linux commands to manage and analyze files and directory permissions. I learned how to access and view files and directory details, identify hidden files, and interpret linux permission strings. This project helped me understand how linux commands and controls were able access to files and directories and why permissions are crucial to system security. 
Check file and directory details
Using the linux command ls -l, I was able to view the files and directories and examined the permissions and ownerships of the files. In order for me to view the file permissions and ownerships of the hidden files, I had to use the command ls -la which shows the hidden files and their permissions. 

Describe the permissions string
I learned how to interpret the linux permission strings, which shows the permissions of three different types of groups: the user/owner, groups, and other users.  After using the command earlier ls -la which showed me the permission of every file in the “projects” directory, it showed me the permission strings of every file. For example “project_k.txt”, showed the permission string of -rw-rw-rw-. The first set of strings: -rw-, shows the permissions of the owner, implying that the owner can read and modify the file. The second string rw- is the group permissions and the third string rw- is the other user permissions.
Change file permissions
After checking the permissions for file “project_k.txt”, I now know that the owner, group, and other users have the permissions to read and modify the file. But we actually don’t want the “other users” to have writing permissions. Using the linux command “chmod o-w project_k.txt”, o standing for other, - standing for remove permission, w standing for writing permission. After sending the command, I used the command ls -la to check again and see if the permission of others is now only reading permissions. 
