Change file permissions on a hidden file
After applying the linux commands to project_k.txt, I also had to change the permissions of the hidden file “.project_x.txt”. Within this file, no one is supposed to have permissions for writing or executing, and only have permission to read this file. I see that in this hidden file, the owner has permission to read and modify, while the group has permission to modify the file as well but doesn’t have reading permissions. I used the linux command “chmod u-w, g=r .project.txt” so that the owner and the group only have reading permissions. The result should look like this after checking permissions to the files again using ls -la.

Change directory permissions
The next task for this project was to change the directory “drafts” permissions of the group. After using the linux command to check the permissions of the directory “drafts” I see that the group has the execute permission meaning they have access to the content. Using the linux command “chmod g-x drafts” I removed the group permission to execute or have access to the contents of this directory. After checking the permissions again, this is what the permission string now looks like for drafts.

Summary
In summary, this project helped me get hands-on experience on learning and using linux commands on files and directories. I was able to inspect and analyze the contents of the files and permissions of the files and directories, be able to analyze contents of hidden files, and modify permissions using the linux commands. These skills taught by this project are important for learning and what it takes to be in the cybersecurity field because it helps prevent unauthorized users from having access to specific files.
